Find all polynomials \( P(x) \) with integer coefficients such that there exist non-constant polynomials \( Q(x) \) and \( R(x) \) with non-negative integer coefficients, and a sequence \( (a_n)_{n \geq 1} \) of integers greater than 1, satisfying the following properties:
- \( 2^{a_n} \leq n \) for every integer \( n \geq 2025 \);
- \( R(n) \) divides \( P\left(a_n^{Q(n)}\right) \) for every integer \( n \geq 2025 \).

A $7 \times 7$ grid is given. Julián colors $29$ cells black. Pilar must then place an $L$-shaped piece, covering exactly three cells (oriented in any direction, as shown in the figure). Pilar wins if all three cells covered by the $L$-shaped piece are black. Can Julián color the grid in such a way that it is impossible for Pilar to win?
[asy]
size(1.5cm);
draw((0,1)--(1,1)--(1,2)--(0,2)--(0,1)--(0,0)--(1,0)--(2,0)--(2,1)--(1,1)--(1,0));
[/asy]

Consider a square $8 \times 8$ board with its $64$ cells initially white. Determine the minimum number of colors needed to color the cells (each one with only one color) in such a way that if four cells on the board can be covered by an $L$-shaped tile as shown in the figure, then the four cells are of different colors.
[asy]
size(3cm);
draw((1,0)--(1,1)--(2,1)--(2,0)--(1,0)--(0,0)--(0,1)--(0,2)--(1,2)--(1,1)--(0,1)--(1,1)--(2,1)--(3,1)--(3,0)--(2,0));
[/asy]
Note: The $L$-shaped tile can be rotated or flipped.

An equilateral triangle with integer side length $n$ is subdivided into smaller equilateral triangles of side length $1$ by drawing lines parallel to its sides, as shown in the figure for $n = 4$.
[asy]
size(5cm);
// Function to draw an equilateral triangle with subdivisions and mark vertices
void drawTriangleWithDots(pair A, pair B, pair C, int n) {
real step = 1.0 / n;
// Draw horizontal lines
for (int i = 0; i &lt;= n; ++i) {
pair start = A + i * step * (C - A);
pair end = start + i * step * (B - C);
draw(start -- end, gray(0.5));
}
// Draw left-leaning diagonal lines
for (int i = 0; i &lt;= n; ++i) {
pair start = A + i * step * (B - A);
pair end = start + (n - i) * step * (C - A);
draw(start -- end, gray(0.5));
}
// Draw right-leaning diagonal lines
for (int i = 0; i &lt;= n; ++i) {
pair start = B + i * step * (C - B);
pair end = start + (n - i) * step * (A - B);
draw(start -- end, gray(0.5));
}
// Mark dots at all vertices
for (int i = 0; i &lt;= n; ++i) {
for (int j = 0; j &lt;= i; ++j) {
pair vertex = A + i * step * (C - A) + j * step * (B - C);
dot(vertex, black);
}
}
// Draw the outer triangle
draw(A -- B -- C -- cycle, black+linewidth(1));
}
// Main triangle vertices
pair A = (0, 0);
pair B = (4, 0);
pair C = (2, 3.464); // Height = sqrt(3)/2 * side length
// Subdivisions
int n = 4;
// Draw the subdivided equilateral triangle with dots
drawTriangleWithDots(A, B, C, n);
[/asy]
Consider the set $A$ consisting of all points that are vertices of any of these smaller triangles. A [i]subtriangle[/i] is defined as any equilateral triangle whose three vertices belong to the set $A$ and whose three sides lie along the lines of the initial subdivision. We wish to color all points in $A$ either red or blue such that no subtriangle has all three vertices of the same color. Let $C(n)$ denote the number of such valid colorings for each positive integer $n$. Calculate, in terms of $n$, the value of $C(n)$.

The game Battleship is played on a $10\times10$ grid. A [i]fleet[/i] consists of 10 ships: one occupying $4$ cells, two occupying $3$ cells each, three occupying $2$ cells each and four occupying $1$ cell each (see figure).
[asy]
size(10cm);
// Function to draw a square centered at a given position
void drawSquare(pair center, real sideLength) {
real halfSide = sideLength / 2;
draw(shift(center) * box((-halfSide, -halfSide), (halfSide, halfSide)));
}
// Side length of each square
real sideLength = 1;
// Coordinates for the squares
pair[] positions = {
// Top row remains the same
(0, 0), (1, 0), (3, 0), (4, 0), (6, 0), (7, 0), (9, 0), (11, 0), (13, 0), (15, 0),
// Bottom row moved one square (1 unit) to the right
(2, 2), (3, 2), (4, 2), (5, 2), (7, 2), (8, 2), (9, 2), (11, 2), (12, 2), (13, 2)
};
// Draw all squares
for (pair pos : positions) {
drawSquare(pos, sideLength);
}
[/asy]
Ships can be placed either horizontally or vertically, but they must not touch each other, not even at a vertex. Is it possible to place two fleets on the same board according to these rules?

Let $p$ be a fixed prime number.
Jomland has $p$ cities labelled $0,1,\dots,p-1$.
Navi is a traveller and JomAirlines only has flights between two cities with labels $a$ and $b$ (flights are available in both directions) iff there exist positive integers $x$ and $y$ such that
\[ \begin{cases}
a \equiv x^2 + 2025xy + y^2\pmod{p}\\
b \equiv 20x^2 + xy + 25y^2\pmod{p}
\end{cases} \]Prove that:
i) There exist infinitely many primes $p$ such that there exist $2$ cities where Navi cannot start from one city and get to the other through a sequence of flights;
ii) There exist infinitely many primes $p$ such that for any $2$ cities, Navi can start from one city and get to the other through a sequence of flights.
[i](Proposed by Ivan Chan Guan Yu)[/i]

Let $n \geq d$ be positive integers. We define the following vector operations in $\mathbb R^d$:
For $X = (x_1, \dots, x_d) \in \mathbb R^d$, we define $\|X\| = \sqrt{\sum_{i = 1}^d x_i^2}$;
For $X = (x_1, \dots, x_d), Y = (y_1, \dots, y_d) \in \mathbb R^d$, we define $X + Y = (x_1 + y_1, \dots, x_d + y_d)$;
For $X = (x_1, \dots, x_d) \in \mathbb R^d$, we say that $X = 0$ if and only if $x_1 = x_2 = \dots = x_d = 0$.
Call a finite nonempty subset $A$ of $\mathbb R^d$ [i]good[/i] if $|X| \leq 1$ for any $X \in A$, and $\sum_{X \in A} X = 0$. Find the least constant $C$ such that for any $n$ good sets $A_1$, $\dots$, $A_n$, there exist $X_1 \in A_1$, $X_2 \in A_2$, $\dots$, $X_n \in A_n$ satisfying $\|X_1 + X_2 + \dots + X_n\| \leq C$.

Let $\mathbb{Z}_+=\{1,2,3,4...\}$ be the set of all positive integers. Determine all functions $f : \mathbb{Z}_+ \mapsto \mathbb{Z}_+$ that satisfy:
$f(mn)+1=f(m)+f(n)$ for all positive integers $m$ and $n$;
$f(2024)=1$;
$f(n)=1$ for all positive $n\equiv22\pmod{23}$.

For positive integers $i,j,k,l$ less than $5$, let $A_{ijkl}$ be an integer. Suppose that the integers $A_{ijkl}$ satisfy the following properties.
(a) $A_{ijkl}=-A_{jikl}$;
(b) $A_{ijkl}=-A_{ijlk}$;
(c) $A_{ijkl}=+A_{klij}$.
What is the maximum number of distinct integers $A_{ijkl}$?
Answer Confirmation$43$

A dot-trapezium consists of several rows of dots such that each row contains one more dot than the row immediately above (apart from the top row). For example here is a dot-trapezium consisting of $15$ dots, having $3$ rows and $4$ dots in the top row.
[asy]
//wonderfully scuffed asymptote code , please don't laugh at me. constructed from the diagram at https://www.mathsolympiad.org.nz/competitions/nzmo/problems/nzmo1_2024.pdf
//top row
dot((.05,.1));
dot((-.05,.1));
dot((-.15,.1));
dot((.15,.1));
//middle row
dot((0,0));
dot((.1,0));
dot((-.1,0));
dot((.2,0));
dot((-.2,0));
//bottom row
dot((.05,-.1));
dot((-.05,-.1));
dot((-.15,-.1));
dot((.15,-.1));
dot((.25,-.1));
dot((-.25,-.1));
[/asy]
A positive integer $n$ is called a trapezium-number if there exists a dot-trapezium consisting of exactly $n$ dots, with at least two rows and at least two dots in the top row. How many trapezium-numbers are there less than $100$?

Let $\mathbb{R}_{
eq 0}$ be the set of nonzero real numbers. Find all functions $f:\mathbb{R}_{
eq 0}ightarrow\mathbb{R}_{
eq 0}$ such that, for all $x,y\in\mathbb{R}_{
eq 0}$, \[(x-y)f(y^2)+f\left(xy\,f\left( rac{x^2}{y}ight)ight)=f(y^2f(y)).\]

Find all $(a, b, c, d)\in \mathbb{R}$ satisfying
\[\begin{aligned}
\begin{cases}
a+b+c+d=0,\\
a^2+b^2+c^2+d^2=12,\\
abcd=-3.\\
\end{cases}
\end{aligned}\]

Find the number of polynomials $F(x,y)$ satisfying the following:
1. $\deg F \leq 2048$;
2. the coefficients of $F(x,y)$ are $0$ and $1$;
3. $F(x,0)=F(0,y)\equiv 0$;
4. $F(x+z,y)+F(z,y)+F(x,y+z)+F(x,z)$ has all of its coefficients being even.
[i]Proposed by usjl[/i]

There are $n$ cities in a country, where $n \geq 100$ is an integer. Some pairs of cities are connected by direct (two-way) flights. For two cities $A$ and $B$ we define:
$(i)$ A $\emph{path}$ between $A$ and $B$ as a sequence of distinct cities $A = C_0, C_1, \dots, C_k, C_{k+1} = B$, $k \geq 0$, such that there are direct flights between $C_i$ and $C_{i+1}$ for every $0 \leq i \leq k$;
$(ii)$ A $\emph{long path}$ between $A$ and $B$ as a path between $A$ and $B$ such that no other path between $A$ and $B$ has more cities;
$(iii)$ A $\emph{short path}$ between $A$ and $B$ as a path between $A$ and $B$ such that no other path between $A$ and $B$ has fewer cities.
Assume that for any pair of cities $A$ and $B$ in the country, there exist a long path and a short path between them that have no cities in common (except $A$ and $B$). Let $F$ be the total number of pairs of cities in the country that are connected by direct flights. In terms of $n$, find all possible values $F$
Proposed by David-Andrei Anghel, Romania.

Let $\triangle ABC$ be a triangle with side‐lengths $a,b,c$, incenter $I$, and circumradius $R$.  Denote by $P$ the area of $\triangle ABC$, and let $P_1,\;P_2,\;P_3$ be the areas of triangles $\triangle ABI$, $\triangle BCI$, and $\triangle CAI$, respectively.  Prove that
\[
\frac{abc}{12R}
\;\le\;
\frac{P_1^2 + P_2^2 + P_3^2}{P}
\;\le\;
\frac{3R^3}{4\sqrt[3]{abc}}.
\]

Let $m$ and $n$ be natural numbers. Every one of the $m*n$ squares of the $m*n$ board is colored either black or white, so that no 2 neighbouring squares are the same color(the board is colored like in chess").In one step we can pick 2 neighbouring squares and change their colors like this:
- a white square becomes black;
-a black square becomes blue;
-a blue square becomes white.
For which $m$ and $n$ can we ,in a finite sequence of these steps, switch the starting colors from white to black and vice versa.

Solve the system of equations in $\mathbb{R}$
\begin{align*}
\frac{a}{c} &amp;= b-\sqrt{b}+c \\
\sqrt{\frac{a}{c}} &amp;= \sqrt{b}+1 \\
\sqrt[4]{\frac{a}{c}} &amp;=\sqrt[3]{b}-1
\end{align*}

For every positive integer $n$ we call $s(n)$ the sum of its digits in the usual base $10$ representation.
\begin{align*}
(a) &amp;\quad \text{Find the smallest positive integer}\quad  n \quad \text{such that}\quad  n=225s(n)\\
(b) &amp;\quad \text{Find the smallest positive integer}\quad  n \quad \text{such that}\quad  n=225s(n)^2\\
\end{align*}

For positive integers $m, t$, let $\operatorname{rad}_t(m)$ denote the product of all distinct prime factors of $m$ that are at most $t$. Write $\operatorname{rad}_{\infty}(m)$ as $\operatorname{rad}(m)$.  Let $p(m)$ denote the largest prime dividing $m$, and let $r(m)$ be the largest prime dividing $m$ such that $\operatorname{rad}_{r(m)}(m) \leq p(m)$. Finally, let $q(m)$ be the smallest prime [i]not[/i] dividing $m$. Suppose $a_0, a_1, a_2, \cdots$ is a sequence of positive integers defined recursively by
\[
a_{n+1}\;=\;
\begin{cases}
a_{n} - \operatorname{rad}(a_{n}) &amp;\text{if }a_{n}&gt;\operatorname{rad}(a_{n})\\[6pt]
a_{n}\cdot(p(a_n) + \operatorname{min}\{q(a_n), r(a_n)\})                       &amp;\text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\]Prove that such a sequence must be eventually periodic.
[i]Proposed by LPC83[/i]

Let \( G \) be a connected graph with \( n \geq 3 \) vertices, where the number of edges is at least \( 2n - 2 \), and the removal of any single vertex leaves the graph connected. Prove that:
There exist two adjacent vertices whose removal leaves \( G \) connected;
There exist two cycles that share exactly one common edge.

Given an even number $m\ge6$.
Say a positive integer sequence $\{a_n\}_{n=1}^{m}$ satisfies property $\tau$, if
(i) $a_1=1$;
(ii) $\forall 1\le k\le m-1$, $a_{k+1}-a_k\in\{a_i\mid 1\le i\le k\}$;
(iii) $a_2,\, a_4,\,\cdots,\, a_m$ is an arithmetic sequence.
(1) If $\{a_n\}_{n=1}^m$ contains $2025$, find the minimum of $m$;
(2) Find the total number of the sequences satisfy $\tau$ when $m=22$.
Hint(1) $1014$
(2) $21221$

Given an even number $m\ge6$.
Say a positive integer sequence $\{a_n\}_{n=1}^{m}$ satisfies property $\tau$, if
(i) $a_1=1$;
(ii) $\forall 1\le k\le m-1$, $a_{k+1}-a_k\in\{a_i\mid 1\le i\le k\}$;
(iii) $a_2,\, a_4,\,\cdots,\, a_m$ is an geometric sequence with common quotient $2$.
(1) Write a $\tau$ sequence when $m=22$;
(2) Find the total number of the sequences satisfy $\tau$ when given $m$.

Let $n\geq 2$, vectors $\vec{v_1},\vec{_2},\cdots ,\vec{v_n}$ satisfy $\sum_{i=1}^n|\vec{v_i}|^2=1$.
Let $M=max\{|\sum_{k=1}^ne_k\vec{v_k}|e_k=\pm1\},N= min\{|\sum_{k=1}^ne_k\vec{v_k}|e_k=\pm1\}$.
Prove: $MN\leq  \begin{cases}
\frac{n}{2\sqrt{n-1}} &amp; \text{if } x \text{ is odd}\\
\frac{n-1}{2\sqrt{n}} &amp; \text{if } x  \text{ is even}
\end{cases}$.

An $n\times n$ square board ($n\ge3$) is initially colored white. In each move we may change to the opposite color all squares forming one of the [i]pentominoes[/i] shown below (each consists of five unit squares).
Determine all $n$ for which it is possible, using such moves, to make all squares black.
[asy]
unitsize(0.8cm);
path sq = box((0,0), (1,1));
unitsize(0.5cm);
path sq = box((0,0),(1,1));
void drawBigT(pair origin, int rotation) {
pair[] coords = {
(0,2), (1,2), (2,2),
(1,1),
(1,0)
};
for (int i = 0; i &lt; coords.length; ++i) {
pair p = origin + rotate(rotation * 90, (1,1)) * coords[i];
draw(shift(p)*sq);
}
}
for (int i = 0; i &lt; 4; ++i) {
drawBigT((i*4,0), i);
}
[/asy]

Find the number of pairs $(a, b)$ of positive integers such that \[
k=\frac{201}{a}+\frac{3}{b}
\]are also an integer.

There are some points on a plane. Two players, A and B, play a game, A starts first. They take turns to play. In each move, a player can draw an edge between two unconnected points, with the rule that no acute triangle can be formed after the move. If a player cannot make a move, they lose the game.
Given an integer \( n \geq 3 \), prove that it is possible to choose \( n \) points on the plane such that:
(1) No three points are collinear;
(2) The number of acute triangles with these points as vertices is at least $\left\lfloor \frac{C_n^3}{100} \right\rfloor$;
(3) Under this configuration, player A has a winning strategy.
[i]Proposed by Site Mu, Beijing 101 Middle School[/i]

Daniel is playing a game on the lattice points. Every second, he makes one of two possible moves:
Place a marker on each unmarked point among $(0,0)$, $(1,0)$, $(0,1)$, and $(1,1)$.
Jump a marker over an orthogonally adjacent marker $\mathcal M$ to an unmarked point, then remove $\mathcal M$ (as illustrated below).
Determine, with proof, the least number of seconds Daniel must take before $(2025,2025)$ is marked.
[asy]
size(6cm);
import graph;
for (int i = 0; i &lt;= 3; ++i) {
draw((0,i)--(4,i), gray(0.6));
}
for (int i = 0; i &lt;= 4; ++i) {
draw((i,0)--(i,3), gray(0.6));
}
pair left = (1,1.5);
pair center = (2,1.5);
pair right = (3,1.5);
filldraw(circle(left, 0.35), gray(0.8), gray(0.3)+linewidth(2));
filldraw(circle(center, 0.35), gray(0.8), gray(0.3)+linewidth(2));
draw(center + (0.25,0.25) -- center + (-0.25,-0.25), linewidth(2));
draw(center + (0.25,-0.25) -- center + (-0.25,0.25), linewidth(2));
draw(circle(right, 0.35), gray(0.5)+dashed);
draw(arc((2,1.5), 1.0, 150, 30), linewidth(1.2), EndArrow(10));
[/asy][i]Benjamin Fu[/i]

A flock of peculiar penchicks peck pickled peanuts, peppers, both, or none. Particularly:
$\frac{1}{3}$ of the penchicks peck pickled peanuts;
$\frac{1}{7}$ of the penchicks peck peppers;
$\frac{1}{12}$ of the penchicks peck pickled peanuts and peppers.
What fraction of the penchick flock pecks neither pickled peanuts nor peppers?
$\text{(a) }\frac{11}{28} \qquad \text{(b) }\frac{17}{28} \qquad \text{(c) } \frac{37}{84} \qquad \text{(d) } \frac{47}{84}$

Let \( n \geq 1 \) be the number of lines drawn in the plane such that no two are parallel and no three are concurrent at a single point. These lines divide the plane into connected regions, both finite and infinite. The figure below is a possible configuration for \( n = 5 \), yielding a total of 16 regions, of which 6 are finite: 4 triangles, 1 quadrilateral, and 1 pentagon.
https://cdn.discordapp.com/attachments/954427908359876608/1429165521542316184/Screenshot_2025-10-18_144251.png?ex=68f5259c&amp;is=68f3d41c&amp;hm=65f717f470de0519598c1f81135df866d6e83a04ad4e676baadd2c307480ce08&amp;
Determine, as a function of \( n \) (the number of lines drawn), the greatest possible number of finite regions with 4 sides (quadrilaterals).

In the country of Polaria, $7$ police officers are catching a thief who is moving between cities. Two cities are called neighbors if they are connected by a direct two-way railway line. Both the police officers and the thief move between cities using railway lines. Every day, first the police officers make one of their moves at the same time, and then the thief makes one of his moves. The police officer can choose to stay in the city he is currently in or go to one of the neighboring cities on his move. After the police officers have made their move, the thief can choose to go to one of the neighboring cities on his move if there are no police officers there at that time, or stay in his current city if there are no police officers there (note that the police officer entering the city where the thief is currently located does not mean that the thief has been caught and the process stops). Both the police officers and the thief know the location of everyone at all times, and the police officers cooperate with each other. A thief is surrounded if he cannot move to any of the neighboring cities during his turn.
Is it possible that there exists in Polaria such an arrangement of cities and railway lines that:
$\bullet$ every city has exactly $7$ neighbors;
$\bullet$ it is impossible to leave and return to a city using fewer than $7$ different railway lines, except in cases where the traveler decides to travel in the opposite direction along one of the railway lines used for departure;
$\bullet$ regardless of which city the thief is in and from which cities the police start the chase, the police can always ensure that the thief is surrounded after a finite number of days?
